> @kungsen : Thank you very much for your sharing. Yes, we can have different possible solutions here. For long term purposes, in my opinion the Choice 3 is the best. I have just 2 concerns. One is once the new "standardized" method is implemented, will all the passes be re-written or they can stay as-is? The other is would the new "standardized" method need the "local type inference"? I think it would still need the "local type inference" since it's would be much cleaner if each pass takes care of the newly generated nodes, because at the graph level, for some cases the total number of nodes is very high (Bert may have ~30k nodes) so that a whole tree walk would be costly. 

@thlinh  I think small adaption of "standardized" method for all pass is acceptable. Before the "standardized" method is ready, we can obey the rule manully to get the gain, as "if ever a subnode is untyped then the root must be untyped" is easy to obey manually. The "local type inference" is still needed.

> @kungsen  I actually don't quite understand your concerns on "redundant computation" in Choice 2 because in my opinion, Choice 1 and Choice 2 should be the same linear complexity (traversal of the subtree). The only reason I think Choice 1 should be preferred is its simplicity in implementation; but Choice 2 makes more sense because its logic is more direct compared to Choice 1. (we are facing problems of untyped nodes).
> I think the "redundancy" only appears in the sense of code duplication.

@endejin  The "redundancy" means that if there is a global level renorm later doing "local type infer" in every pass is reduntant. If we can collect all untyped nodes, and only "local type infer" need to be done would be prefered.

> @endejin  Thank you for your detailed explaination. I am still uncertain if this suit for our IR system. For example, "use recursive style/immutable style IRMutator/IRFunctor so that when replacing with an untyped node, all the parents of the untyped node will change to untyped", this may be unacceptable for AD and AutoParallel transformation.

@gongchen123 123 123 123  Hi, Thanks for your comments! Lately I have been trying to take time to read the new AD. Correct me if I am wrong, but I don't see any mutation on primal_graph happens in `Grad` and `DFunctor`? The Algorithm is keeping creating new nodes for k_graph, thus it is actually **immutable**; what's more, the key part MapMorphism is actually a **recursive visiting**, which means if written in visitor pattern, the code will be much clearer and concise; the imperative style of visiting Parameter Node/ Value Node is just a base case.

So it is actually a recursive immutable pass, but written in a not concise way. Actually this reflects that a lot of transformations can be easily and naturally written in recursive/immutable style -- easy to understand, simple to ensure correctness and reason (the "Typed"-Tag's definition has demonstrated this advantage) and less error-prone.

06/26/2020 Modification: 
Based on this RFC, we now have the first step of implementing the base class `distribution`, and two example concrete distributions: `Normal` and `Bernoulli`. 
Our current design is different from the RFC or something gets more concrete, main in these aspects:
1.`Multi-output compile and run`: 
One distribution is a library, not an op nor a real cell, so it has lots of methods for users to call, as mentioned in the RFC. Since a distribution is a fake `cell`, it is conventionally required to use `construct` to call the distribution. Method invocation doesn't support a way like `normal.pdf(*inputs)`. One assuming way to solve this problem is modifying the `compile_and_run_method` function, but it was proved not applicable as far as we tried. 

However, we now decide to implement this based in if-else selection way, spliting the `construct` into different parts using selection statements: 
```
def construct(self, *inputs):
    if inputs[0] == 'log_prob':
        return self._call_log_prob(*inputs)
    if inputs[0] == 'prob':
        return self._call_prob(*inputs)
    if inputs[0] == 'kl_loss':
        return self._kl_loss(*inputs)
    if inputs[0] == 'mean':
        return self._mean()
    if inputs[0] == 'sd':
        return self._call_sd()
    if inputs[0] == 'sample':
        return self._sample(*inputs)
    return None
```
The method-corresponding name string is introduced in this way. That is, we also pass the name as the first parameter part into distribution's construct, then the construct method would decide which sub-method to call based on the name.
2. `Method Wrapper`:
Instead of checking the whether the class has the attribute at run-time, we set the method of computation during the initialization phase of a distribution instance. At the end of Distribution’s `__init__ `, we call wrappers such as `_set_prob()`, which will help construct to find the correct available function. Based on whether the derived class has the attribute `_prob` or `_log_likelihood`, we will set `self._call_prob` to `self._prob` or `self._calc_prob_from_log_prob`. 
This change was made for the reason that python built-in functions such as `hasattr` are not supported by Mindspore parser. To avoid using `hasattr`, we do the setting in `__init__`. However, the current method is not very intuitive. We hope to find a more intuitive way after fixing the problem with parser.
```
  def _set_prob(self):
    """
    Set probability funtion based on the availability of _prob and _log_likehood.
    """
    if hasattr(self, '_prob'):
        self._call_prob = self._prob
    elif hasattr(self, '_log_likelihood'):
        self._call_prob = self._calc_prob_from_log_likelihood

```
